questions.explanation:
For a signal\[s(t)=A_c\cos\theta(t),\]the instantaneous frequency is \[f_i(t)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{d\theta(t)}{dt}.\]For the PM signal, \[S_{PM}(t)=\cos\!\left(1000\pi t+K_p m(t)\right),\]the instantaneous frequency is \[f_{i,PM}
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\frac{d}{dt}
\left(1000\pi t+K_p m(t)\right)
=
500+\frac{K_p}{2\pi}\frac{dm(t)}{dt}.\]Hence, \[(f_{i,PM})_{\max}
=
500+\frac{K_p}{2\pi}
\left(\frac{dm(t)}{dt}\right)_{\max}.\]From the given waveform, \[m(t)
\text{ rises from }0\text{ V to }10\text{ V}
\text{ in }(3-1)=2\text{ s}.\]Therefore, \[\left(\frac{dm(t)}{dt}\right)_{\max}
=
\frac{10-0}{3-1}
=
5.\]Thus, \[(f_{i,PM})_{\max}
=
500+\frac{5K_p}{2\pi}.\]For the FM signal, \[S_{FM}(t)=
\cos\!\left(
1000\pi t+
K_f\int_{-\infty}^{t}m(\tau)\,d\tau
\right),\]the instantaneous frequency is \[f_{i,FM}
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\frac{d}{dt}
\left(
1000\pi t+
K_f\int_{-\infty}^{t}m(\tau)\,d\tau
\right).\]Using \[\frac{d}{dt}
\left(
\int_{-\infty}^{t}m(\tau)\,d\tau
\right)
=
m(t),\]we obtain \[f_{i,FM}
=
500+\frac{K_f}{2\pi}m(t).\]The maximum value of \(m(t)\) from the graph is \[m_{\max}=10.\]Hence, \[(f_{i,FM})_{\max}
=
500+\frac{10K_f}{2\pi}.\]Given that the highest instantaneous frequencies are equal, \[(f_{i,PM})_{\max}
=
(f_{i,FM})_{\max}.\]Therefore, \[500+\frac{5K_p}{2\pi}
=
500+\frac{10K_f}{2\pi}.\]Cancelling common terms, \[5K_p=10K_f.\]Hence, \[\boxed{\frac{K_p}{K_f}=2\ \text{s}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>For PM, \[f_i(t)=f_c+\frac{K_p}{2\pi}\frac{dm(t)}{dt},\] so the instantaneous frequency depends on the slope of \(m(t)\).</li><li>For FM, \[f_i(t)=f_c+\frac{K_f}{2\pi}m(t),\] so the instantaneous frequency depends on the amplitude of \(m(t)\).</li><li>Do not use \(m_{\max}\) for PM; PM requires \[\left(\frac{dm(t)}{dt}\right)_{\max}.\]</li><li>From a piecewise-linear waveform, compute slope as \[\frac{\Delta m}{\Delta t},\] not from the peak value directly.</li></ul></div>
